\title{\sys{}: Single-Node Speed That Scales\\
       \large One Mergeable Algebra from a Core to a Cluster}

\author{Stephen Offer\\ \small Anyscale \quad\textbullet\quad \texttt{stephen.offer@anyscale.com}}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
The data engines most teams run today were designed for a world that no longer
exists. Spark's architecture assumes a machine too small to hold the problem, so
it distributes everything and pays for it on every query. DuckDB and Polars
assume the opposite and are wonderfully fast until the day the data outgrows the
box. Both families optimize a query once, before it runs, from statistics that
were stale before the query was written. And all of them treat machine learning
as somebody else's job---a second system, a second copy of the data, and a
serialization boundary in between.

\sys{} is built on three different bets.

\textbf{One algebra, every scale.} Every stateful operator exists exactly once,
as a mergeable triple \pcf{} in Rust over Apache Arrow. One core, ninety-six
cores, and a cluster differ only in how that triple is \emph{scheduled}. Small
queries pay nothing for distribution, because distribution is a decision made per
query rather than a runtime you start. Large queries need no rewrite, because the
operator that wins on a laptop is the operator that runs on the cluster.

\textbf{An engine that learns.} \sys{} measures what every operator actually
did---rows, time, CPU, peak bytes---and plans the next query from that evidence
instead of from constants a vendor chose years ago. Cost coefficients are refit
from observed runtimes. Join strategies are chosen by a bandit over past
outcomes. Memory is sized from footprints operators really used. Every learned
value is provably result-invariant, so learning can be aggressive without ever
risking an answer.

\textbf{One engine for the whole pipeline.} Relational queries, image and audio
decode, embeddings, vector search, GPU batch inference, and lakehouse writes are
all expressions over the same Arrow batches, in the same plan, under the same
optimizer. The tensor never leaves the engine to be processed.

The results bracket both ends. On one 96-core machine \sys{} matches DuckDB on
TPC-H~\cite{tpch} sf1, leads Polars and Daft by $1.8$--$2.2\times$, sweeps
PyArrow's Acero engine, and wins 43 of 43 ClickBench queries against DuckDB on
identical input. On a cluster, aggregate shuffle throughput rises
\emph{superlinearly}: $7.6\times$ for $4\times$ the nodes. We are equally clear
about what is not yet done, because a roadmap is more useful than a scoreboard.
\end{abstract}

% ===========================================================================
\section{The assumptions that expired}
% ===========================================================================

Every architecture encodes the world it was born into. The distributed data stack
most companies run was designed between 2009 and 2014, and it made three
assumptions that were completely reasonable then and are quietly wrong now.

\textbf{``The data will not fit on one machine.''} In 2012 a large server had 16
cores and 64\,GB of memory, so the only way to process a terabyte was to spread
it across a rack. Everything followed from that: a cluster you start before you
ask a question, a scheduler between you and your data, and serialization on every
hop. Today a single cloud instance has 96 to 192 cores and one to twelve
terabytes of memory. The machine that needed a cluster now fits in a footnote.
But the tax remained---which is why a query that would take twelve milliseconds
in-process still takes several seconds through a distributed runtime.

\textbf{``We know what the data looks like.''} Classical optimizers were built
for warehouses with curated schemas, table statistics refreshed on a schedule,
and queries written once and run for years. Optimize once, before execution, from
the catalog. That world had \texttt{ANALYZE} jobs and DBAs. The modern one has
schema drift, object storage, JSON columns, and a query that never sees the same
data distribution twice. Optimizing once from stale statistics is not a
performance choice anymore; it is a guess.

\textbf{``Analytics and machine learning are separate systems.''} They were, for
good reasons: the tools were different, the hardware was different, the teams
were different. So the standard pipeline is a warehouse query that writes
Parquet, a second framework that reads it back, a GPU cluster that reads it
again, and a serialization boundary at every seam. Most of the wall-clock time in
a modern AI pipeline is not computation. It is copying.

