\centering\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.15}
\begin{tabular}{p{0.24\textwidth} p{0.30\textwidth} p{0.38\textwidth}}
\toprule
\textbf{The 2010s assumption} & \textbf{What changed} & \textbf{\sys{}'s answer} \\
\midrule
One machine is too small, so distribute everything and pay the tax always.
& A single node now has 96--192 cores and terabytes of RAM. Most queries never
  needed a cluster; some still do.
& \win One mergeable algebra. Distribution is a per-query scheduling decision,
  not a runtime. No tax on the small case, no rewrite on the large one. \\
Optimize once, before execution, from catalog statistics.
& Schemas drift, storage is object storage, and the same query never sees the
  same data twice.
& \win Plan from what was \emph{measured}: sketches built during execution, cost
