\end{tabular}
\caption{Why a new architecture, rather than a faster implementation of the old
one. Each row is a design decision the previous generation made correctly for its
era, and a consequence of that decision which is now the thing slowing teams
down.}
\label{tab:generations}
\end{table*}

None of those three assumptions holds. Yet the engines built on them are still
the default, and the workarounds have become folklore: keep a cluster warm so
small queries are bearable; hand-tune the shuffle partition count; maintain a
second copy of the data for the ML team. \cref{tab:generations} is this paper's
argument in one page.

% ===========================================================================
\section{Bet one: one algebra, every scale}
\label{sec:algebra}
% ===========================================================================

Ask an engine builder how a \texttt{GROUP BY} works and you get two answers. On
one machine it is a hash table. On a cluster it is a shuffle, a partial pass, and
a reduce. The two share a name, a SQL semantics, and not a line of code.

That is the root of the bifurcation. Two implementations means two performance
profiles, two sets of bugs, and eventually two different answers---a float key
that hashes one way locally and another after a shuffle splits one group in two,
and the single-node test suite stays green because the single-node implementation
is correct.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
% ---------- LEFT: the conventional design --------------------------------
\begin{scope}
  \node[pfig/title] at (0,1.5) {The old way: two implementations};
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=26mm] (op1) at (-1.8,0.5) {Local operator\\\scriptsize hash table};
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=26mm] (op2) at ( 1.8,0.5) {Distributed operator\\\scriptsize shuffle + reduce};
  \node[pfig/io,  minimum width=18mm] (r1)  at (-1.8,-1.15) {Result A};
  \node[pfig/io,  minimum width=18mm] (r2)  at ( 1.8,-1.15) {Result B};
  \draw[pfig/arrow] (op1) -- (r1);
  \draw[pfig/arrow] (op2) -- (r2);
  \draw[<->, line width=0.9pt, draw=okVermillion, dashed]
        (r1) -- node[pfig/label]{\scriptsize\bfseries\color{okVermillion} may differ} (r2);
  \node[font=\scriptsize, text=pfigGray, align=center] at (0,-1.95)
        {rewrite to scale $\cdot$ divergence found in production};
\end{scope}
% ---------- RIGHT: Batcher ------------------------------------------------
\begin{scope}[xshift=92mm]
  \node[pfig/title] at (0,1.5) {\sys{}: one algebra, three schedulers};
  \node[pfig/new, minimum width=40mm] (tri) at (0,0.5)
        {mergeable triple\\[1pt]\scriptsize\mdseries \pcf{}};
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=18mm] (s1) at (-2.6,-1.15) {1 core\\\scriptsize fold};
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=18mm] (s2) at ( 0.0,-1.15) {$n$ cores\\\scriptsize morselize};
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=18mm] (s3) at ( 2.6,-1.15) {$m$ nodes\\\scriptsize shuffle};
  \draw[pfig/arrowNew] (tri) -- (s1);
  \draw[pfig/arrowNew] (tri) -- (s2);
  \draw[pfig/arrowNew] (tri) -- (s3);
  \node[font=\scriptsize, text=pfigGray, align=center] at (0,-1.95)
        {same code at every scale $\cdot$ equivalence is a property test};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The decision the rest of the system is built on. \sys{} admits an
operator only if it can be written as a mergeable triple---a partial pass, an
associative and commutative combine, and a finalize---and then varies only the
\emph{scheduling}. Aggregation, distinct, join build, window, and top-$N$ all
qualify. There is no second implementation to disagree with the first, so scaling
up is a configuration change rather than a migration.}
\label{fig:fork}
\end{figure*}

\sys{} makes that split impossible. A stateful operator may exist only as a
mergeable triple, and the three execution regimes vary only its scheduling
(\cref{fig:fork}). One core folds a stream of morsels. Many cores morselize and
merge. Many machines partition, run the partial pass per node, ship
pre-aggregated state, and combine in a tree. Formally, for any partitioning
$\{p_k\}$ of an input $P$,
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{combine\_finalize}\big(\textstyle\bigcup_k \mathsf{partial}(p_k)\big)
  = \mathsf{finalize}(\mathsf{partial}(P)),
\label{eq:merge}
\end{equation}
which is not a comment but a property test: a generator produces random
partitionings and asserts equality against the single-pass result, and a separate
test asserts that a distributed run and a single-node run of the same plan return
identical rows.

What this buys a user is simple to state. A query that fits one machine runs
in-process with no cluster, no scheduler, and no serialization---which is why
\sys{} matches DuckDB on TPC-H sf1 rather than paying the distributed tax on
twelve-millisecond queries. The same query, unchanged, runs on eight nodes when
the data grows. And because mappers publish state that is already reduced rather
than raw rows, per-node memory stays bounded however wide the cluster gets, which
is why shuffle throughput \emph{grows} with node count instead of collapsing into
contention (\cref{fig:scaling}).

To be clear about what is and is not new here: the mergeable triple itself is
old. It is Gray et al.'s algebraic aggregate~\cite{gray1997datacube} and
MapReduce's combiner~\cite{dean2004mapreduce}, and every distributed engine
reaches for it somewhere, usually for aggregation. What is different is treating
it as an \emph{admission criterion} for every stateful operator---join build,
window, top-$N$ included---so that no second implementation ever exists to drift
from the first.

The constraint has a price and we pay it honestly: an algorithm with no mergeable
form does not get added. \cref{sec:mechanisms} names the operator where that
still costs us.

% ===========================================================================
\section{Bet two: an engine that learns}
\label{sec:learning-intro}
% ===========================================================================

Here is the second consequence of refusing to fork, and it is the one that
compounds. Because the same operator runs everywhere, \textbf{a measurement taken
anywhere is valid everywhere}. A cardinality observed on one core is evidence
about the same operator on ninety-six. That is what makes it sound for the engine
to plan from its own history.

Every other engine in \cref{tab:differs} ships its cost model as constants,
chooses join strategy by heuristic, and sizes memory from a plan estimate. Those
constants were tuned once, on somebody else's hardware, against somebody else's
data. \sys{} replaces each of them with a measurement:

\begin{itemize}\itemsep1pt\parskip0pt\topsep2pt
\item \textbf{Cardinality} comes from sketches built during execution and from
      the true row counts of previous runs---not from a catalog someone forgot
      to refresh.
\item \textbf{Cost coefficients} are refit from observed operator times, so the
      model describes the machine the query is actually running on.
\item \textbf{Physical strategy}---hash, broadcast, or sort-merge join---is
      chosen by a bandit over measured outcomes, so the engine converges on
      whichever wins for \emph{this} shape on \emph{this} hardware.
\item \textbf{Memory} is sized from each operator family's real footprint, so
      spill decisions reflect what operators use rather than what a planner
      guessed.
\end{itemize}

One rule governs all of it, and it is what makes the idea safe rather than
alarming: \textbf{every learned value is result-invariant}. Each bandit arm emits
the same relation. A threshold picks between equivalent algorithms. A partition
count only shards. Learning changes how much memory a query reserves, when it
spills, how wide a morsel is, and which of several equivalent algorithms runs---
and it can never change the answer. That is property-tested, and a cold store
reproduces the untuned engine byte for byte. The worst a stale learned value can
do is cost throughput.

This is a deliberately different bet from learned query
optimizers~\cite{marcus2019neo,marcus2021bao,kipf2019learned}, which train models
to predict good plans. Those change which plan runs in ways that are hard to
bound. \sys{} confines learning to choices that are provably equivalent, which
buys a weaker signal and a far stronger safety property---and means the feature
can ship on by default. \cref{sec:kyber,sec:carbonite} are the two subsystems
that implement it.

% ===========================================================================
\section{Bet three: one engine for the whole pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}
% ===========================================================================

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
% --- old: multi-system --------------------------------------------------
\node[pfig/title, anchor=west] at (-40,10) {\scriptsize Conventional: three systems, two copies};
\node[pfig/box, minimum width=16mm, font=\scriptsize] (a1) at (-30,2) {SQL engine};
\node[pfig/io,  minimum width=13mm, font=\scriptsize] (f1) at (-11,2) {Parquet};
\node[pfig/box, minimum width=16mm, font=\scriptsize] (a2) at ( 8,2) {ML framework};
\node[pfig/io,  minimum width=13mm, font=\scriptsize] (f2) at (26,2) {Parquet};
\node[pfig/box, minimum width=16mm, font=\scriptsize] (a3) at (44,2) {GPU cluster};
\draw[pfig/arrow] (a1)--(f1); \draw[pfig/arrow] (f1)--(a2);
\draw[pfig/arrow] (a2)--(f2); \draw[pfig/arrow] (f2)--(a3);
\node[font=\scriptsize, text=okVermillion] at (-11,-4.5) {serialize};
\node[font=\scriptsize, text=okVermillion] at (26,-4.5) {serialize};
% --- new: one plan --------------------------------------------------------
\node[pfig/title, anchor=west] at (-40,-16) {\scriptsize \sys{}: one plan, one optimizer, zero copies};
\node[pfig/new, minimum width=17mm, font=\scriptsize] (b1) at (-30,-24) {read \& filter};
\node[pfig/new, minimum width=17mm, font=\scriptsize] (b2) at (-8,-24) {decode\\\scriptsize image / audio};
\node[pfig/new, minimum width=17mm, font=\scriptsize] (b3) at (14,-24) {embed\\\scriptsize / infer (GPU)};
\node[pfig/new, minimum width=17mm, font=\scriptsize] (b4) at (36,-24) {join \& write\\\scriptsize lakehouse};
\draw[pfig/arrowNew] (b1)--(b2); \draw[pfig/arrowNew] (b2)--(b3); \draw[pfig/arrowNew] (b3)--(b4);
\node[font=\scriptsize, text=okBlue] at (3,-31) {same Arrow batches, one optimized plan};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Why the seams cost more than the compute. In the conventional stack each
handoff is a full serialize, write, read, and deserialize of the whole dataset,
and no optimizer sees across the boundary. In \sys{} the decode, the embedding,
and the inference are expressions in the same plan as the filter and the join, so
a predicate can be pushed below a decode and a tensor never leaves the engine.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

The third bet is the one most visible to a user. In \sys{}, preparing data for a
model is not a different kind of work from querying it.

Image decode, resize, center-crop, and normalization to a tensor are Arrow
kernels in the data plane, not a Python UDF. So are audio decode, resampling,
mel-spectrogram, and MFCC---the last two matched to \texttt{torchaudio} within
$10^{-6}$, and, among the general-purpose engines we surveyed, they have no
in-engine equivalent anywhere else. So are vector distances, embedding pooling,
MinHash and SimHash signatures, and fuzzy string matching. Vector search runs in
SQL: \texttt{ORDER BY list\_cosine\_similarity(emb, \dots) LIMIT k} is an
ordinary query. Batch inference runs against session-warm actor pools that load a
model once and reuse it across executions, with CPU decode and GPU compute
overlapped as separate stages so the accelerator is not idle while a JPEG is
being unpacked.

The consequence is \cref{fig:pipeline}. A retrieval pipeline---read a corpus,
strip HTML, chunk it, embed it, write to a lakehouse table---is one plan, one
optimizer, and one pass over the data. So is a multimodal one: read images from
object storage, decode and normalize them, run a model, join the predictions
against a customer table, and \texttt{MERGE} the result into Delta. The filter
that removes 90\% of the rows runs \emph{before} the decode, because the
optimizer can see both.

We want to be precise about the claim, because it is easy to overstate. Others
can do each of these things; Ray Data and Daft in particular have strong
multimodal surfaces, and Daft is the closest comparison. What is unusual here is
that they are all \emph{expressions in one optimized plan} over one columnar
format, rather than stages in different systems joined by files---and that the
same optimizer, the same memory manager, and the same mergeable algebra govern
the image decode and the hash join alike. \cref{sec:limits} lists what this
surface does not yet include.
% ===========================================================================
\section{Architecture}
\label{sec:arch}
% ===========================================================================

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[pfig/io,  minimum width=30mm] (user) at (0,2.55) {Python API / SQL};
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=58mm] (plan) at (0,1.75)
        {\texttt{plan} --- LogicalPlan, Expr, JSON IR};
  \node[pfig/alt, minimum width=17mm] (ky) at (-1.95,0.75) {\kyber{}\\\scriptsize\emph{decides}};
  \node[pfig/alt, minimum width=17mm] (ca) at ( 0.00,0.75) {\carbo{}\\\scriptsize\emph{protects}};
  \node[pfig/alt, minimum width=17mm] (co) at ( 1.95,0.75) {Core\\\scriptsize\emph{measures}};
  \node[pfig/store, minimum width=15mm] (hub) at (-1.95,-0.55) {\scriptsize Metadata Hub};
  \node[pfig/new,  minimum width=58mm] (rust) at (0,-1.75)
        {Rust data plane over Arrow\\[1pt]\scriptsize\mdseries \pcf{} in \texttt{bc-runtime}};

  \draw[pfig/arrow] (user) -- (plan);
  \draw[pfig/arrow] (plan.south -| ky.north) -- (ky.north);
  \draw[pfig/arrow] (ky) -- node[pfig/label]{\scriptsize cost} (ca);
  \draw[pfig/arrow] (ca) -- node[pfig/label]{\scriptsize credits} (co);
  \draw[pfig/arrowNew] (co.south) to[out=270,in=0]
        node[pfig/label,pos=0.55]{\scriptsize JSON IR $\downarrow$ \, Arrow $\uparrow$} (rust.east);
  \draw[pfig/arrowD] (co.south west) to[out=250,in=0]
        node[pfig/label,pos=0.65]{\scriptsize measured} (hub.east);
  \draw[pfig/arrowD] (hub.north) -- node[pfig/label]{\scriptsize learned} (ky.south);
  \begin{scope}[on background layer]
    \node[pfig/lane, fit=(user)(plan)(ky)(ca)(co)(hub),
          label={[pfig/title,anchor=south west,yshift=0.4mm]north west:\scriptsize Control plane (Python)}] {};
    \node[pfig/lane, fit=(rust)] {};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The contract loop. Python builds and optimizes a plan and ships it as
JSON IR; all per-row work happens in Rust over Arrow and returns zero-copy. Core
\emph{measures}, \kyber{} \emph{decides}, \carbo{} \emph{protects}---and the
three cannot import one another, enforced on every commit. That lint is what
stops the optimizer from quietly collecting its own telemetry, which compiles and
passes tests while corrupting the loop.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

\sys{} is a Python control plane over a Rust data plane, with Arrow
\texttt{RecordBatch} the only columnar contract at every internal boundary and
across the FFI: 97{,}389 lines of Rust across 14 crates, 228{,}164 of Python.
Python builds a \texttt{LogicalPlan}, optimizes it, lowers it to JSON whose
operator tags are the \texttt{serde} tags of the Rust \texttt{RelOp} enum, and
hands it across. Data returns zero-copy through the Arrow C Data Interface.
Python never iterates a row, which lets the control plane be a high-level
language while nothing it does sits on a per-row path.

The three subsystems of \cref{fig:arch} are mutually isolated by a lint contract.
The rule reads as bureaucratic until you see what it prevents: a \kyber{} pass
that collects runtime metadata, or a Core path that makes an optimization
decision, compiles and passes every test while silently corrupting the feedback
loop the design depends on.

Two execution tiers share one expression type. The sequential interpreter is the
correctness oracle; a Cranelift~\cite{cranelift} JIT compiles the numeric,
non-nullable subset and must be bit-for-bit identical on it, falling back
silently otherwise. Mid-development, one commit switched the interpreter's
integer arithmetic to checked while the JIT still wrapped---the same expression
returning a wrapped negative or an error depending on whether the JIT happened to
compile it. Nothing about that is visible in a result. The differential test that
runs both tiers on one input went red immediately.

