\section{How \sys{} compares}
\label{sec:differs}
% ===========================================================================

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lllllll}
\toprule
 & \textbf{\sys{}} & DuckDB & Polars & Spark & Ray Data & Daft \\
\midrule
Single-node / distributed & \win one algebra & single-node & single-node & two paths & two paths & two paths \\
Data plane & \win Arrow + Flight & Arrow (local) & Arrow (local) & JVM rows & object store & Arrow + Ray \\
Optimizer & \win 375 rules, bushy DP & rules + DP & rules & Catalyst & \loss none & rules \\
Cardinality from & \win sketches + measured & table stats & estimates & table stats & \loss --- & estimates \\
Cost model & \win refit from runtime & static & static & static & \loss --- & static \\
Physical strategy & \win bandit over past runs & heuristic & heuristic & heuristic & \loss --- & heuristic \\
Memory sizing & \win learned per-row & static & static & static & static & static \\
Re-optimizes mid-query & stage boundary & \loss no & \loss no & stage boundary (AQE) & \loss no & \loss no \\
In-engine image ops & \win native kernels & \loss none & \loss none & \loss none & UDF & native \\
In-engine audio / mel & \win native kernels & \loss none & \loss none & \loss none & \loss UDF & \loss UDF \\
Vector search in SQL & \win yes & \loss no & \loss no & \loss no & \loss no & \loss no \\
GPU batch inference & \win warm pools & \loss none & \loss none & external & actor pools & actor pools \\
Spill & agg / join / sort & agg / join / sort & partial & \win full + disk shuffle & object store & partial \\
Native store & \loss none (Arrow only) & \win compressed & none & Parquet & none & none \\
String representation & \loss no StringView & \win StringView & \win StringView & UTF8 & --- & --- \\
Streaming guarantees & \loss micro-batch & \loss none & \loss none & micro-batch & \loss none & \loss none \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Blue marks a capability no other column has; amber marks a gap. The
distinctive block is the middle: everything derived from measured runs rather
than shipped as a constant, and a native AI surface inside the same optimized
plan. The distinctive \emph{deficit} is storage---no compressed native format and
no string view---and \cref{sec:eval} shows it landing exactly where this table
predicts.}
\label{tab:differs}
\end{table*}

Four systems bracket the space. \textbf{DuckDB}~\cite{raasveldt2019duckdb} is the
single-node bar: vectorized, a compressed native store with zone maps, optimized
once before execution, and it does not distribute.
\textbf{Spark}~\cite{zaharia2012rdd,armbrust2015sparksql} distributes, and its
Adaptive Query Execution~\cite{sparkaqe} re-plans on measured statistics at stage
boundaries; its unit of movement is a row-shuffle over JVM serialization, and its
fixed per-query cost is large. \textbf{Ray Data}~\cite{raydata}, on
Ray~\cite{moritz2018ray}, targets ML pipelines and has no relational optimizer.
\textbf{Daft}~\cite{daft} and \textbf{Polars}~\cite{polars} are native Rust
engines over Arrow~\cite{arrow}; Daft distributes over Ray, Polars does not.

\cref{tab:differs} places \sys{} against all of them, and the pattern in it is
the paper's thesis in tabular form. \sys{} does not lead because its kernels are
faster than everyone else's---on several rows it is at parity, and on storage it
is behind. It leads on the rows where the previous generation shipped a constant
and \sys{} ships a measurement, and on the rows where the previous generation
required a second system.

