\caption{\kyber{}'s seven phases and where measured evidence enters. The three
rewrite phases iterate to a fixpoint (dashed self-loops) because their rules are
confluent; the cost-based and physical phases run once, because they make a
decision instead of converging to one. Everything on the right is derived from
past executions and defaults to the shipped constant on a cold store.}
\label{fig:kyber}
\end{figure}

\subsection{375 rules, and why that is safe}

Rules are grouped by family---boolean and Kleene algebra, sargability, temporal
extraction, predicate inference, join shape, set operations, window
canonicalization, and expression families grouped by value domain---and
discovered by a decorator registry. A rule set this size is safe only if each
rule preserves results and no two interfere, and \kyber{} proves both
mechanically. Each rule carries a plan-shape unit test and a differential test
against DuckDB across nulls, empties, and type edges. The set as a whole is
property-tested: a generator builds a random table and a random valid pipeline
and asserts that the result under all 375 rules equals the result under none,
order-independent. The same suite proves confluence and termination---the set
converges to a deterministic fixpoint inside the production iteration budget, so
the rules provably do not oscillate.

Two invariants bound what the algebraic families may do, and both have bitten.
Integer arithmetic wraps on overflow, bit-for-bit with the JIT, so reassociation
may only reduce $=$ and $\neq$---bijections of $\mathbb{Z}/2^{64}$, hence
wrap-invariant---and never an ordered comparison, since wrapping breaks
monotonicity. Three-valued logic means every boolean law is proven over
$\{\top,\bot,\mathrm{N}\}$; $x \wedge \neg x \rightarrow \bot$ fires only on
never-null predicates. Some identities that look safe are refused, each refusal
pinned by a test: $\mathrm{pow}(x,1)$ is not folded, because libm's \texttt{pow}
is not correctly rounded and the identity is therefore not provable, while
$\mathrm{pow}(x,0)$ is folded because IEEE 754 specifies it as exactly one for
every base. No timezone-conversion rewrite exists at all---a same-zone conversion
is not the identity, because the engine nulls DST-ambiguous and nonexistent local
times, and dropping the call would resurrect those rows' values.

\subsection{Provenance: rules gated on proof, not confidence}
\label{sec:provenance}

Every cardinality and column statistic carries \emph{where it came from}, and the
distinction between proven-exact and estimated-however-confidently is
load-bearing rather than informational.

Two rule families fire only on \texttt{EXACT} provenance. One drops a
\texttt{Distinct} over a column proven unique, skips a \texttt{Sort} over at most
one row, prunes a constant sort key, and decides a column-to-column comparison
from both columns' proven bounds. The other folds a subtree an exact cardinality
proves empty. A sketch estimate may never drive either, because dropping a
\texttt{Distinct} on a wrong guess is silent data corruption: the query returns
quickly, with duplicate rows, and nothing turns red. Everything else in the
optimizer may consume an estimate, because the worst outcome there is a slower
plan.

Provenance also names a live defect. The gate that turns on adaptive staging
reads provenance as a proxy for ``we do not know this size.'' After the seeding
fix below, TPC-H q5's operands are estimated to within $1.0\times$ of actual and
\emph{still} carry the default provenance label, because the one-shot path never
records an intermediate join's measured cardinality. A label that has diverged
from the evidence it stands for is the characteristic failure of this design;
\cref{sec:limits} reports what it costs.

\subsection{Cardinality: four sources, strict precedence}

Estimation starts from Selinger-style
selectivities~\cite{selinger1979access}---equality passes 10\% of rows, a range
predicate a third, \texttt{IS NULL} 5\%---and is superseded in fixed order by
exact footer metadata, then by sketches built during execution
(HyperLogLog~\cite{flajolet2007hyperloglog} for distinct counts,
KLL~\cite{karnin2016kll} for quantiles, Count-Min~\cite{cormode2005countmin} for
heavy hitters), then by measured cardinalities recorded under a plan signature
(\cref{fig:kyber}). Every sketch is mergeable with a fixed seed, so one built per
partition merges identically to one built on a single node---the algebra of
\cref{sec:algebra} applied to statistics.

\paragraph{Seeding the estimate the join order depends on.} TPC-H
\texttt{orders.o\_orderkey} and \texttt{customer.c\_nationkey} carry no distinct
count in any footer. Without one the join estimator falls back to the
primary-key/foreign-key assumption $\max(|L|,|R|)$---right for a
fact-to-dimension join, catastrophic for a many-to-many key. At sf10 that priced
$\mathit{customer} \bowtie \mathit{supplier}$ on \texttt{nationkey} at
1{,}500{,}000 rows, the left side's row count, where the true output is
$1.5\text{M} \times 20{,}037/5 \approx 6.0$ billion. The executor set about
materializing it and passed 110\,GB resident. \kyber{} now runs an HLL pass to
seed the distinct count \emph{before} the optimize that fixes join order for the
whole query; the pass is idempotent and shared with per-stage refinement, so it
replaces a blind pass instead of adding one. Seeded, the planner picks the order
DuckDB picks, and q5 falls from $8.81\times$ to $3.95\times$ of DuckDB.

\subsection{Cost, refit from runtime}

Three axes---CPU, I/O, network---collapse to one scalar, with network shuffle
bytes weighted at twice local bytes. Per-operator coefficients (inserting into a
hash table costs more than probing it) are \emph{recalibrated from measured
operator times} once enough samples accumulate, clamped so timing noise cannot
produce a degenerate model. Join ordering is exact dynamic programming over bushy
plans at or below 12 tables, greedy to 25, unordered above that.

\subsection{Two learned primitives}
\label{sec:kyber-learned}

All of \kyber{}'s self-tuning reduces to two reusable pieces. Both read $O(1)$
sufficient statistics from the hub, keyed by plan signature and scoped to the
host's hardware profile, so a value learned on a 16-core laptop never steers a
96-core planner.

\textbf{A deterministic UCB1 bandit}~\cite{auer2002ucb} selects over a fixed arm
set from measured per-arm latencies. There is no RNG and ties break by arm name,
which matters more here than in a typical bandit application: a nondeterministic
optimizer makes every performance regression unfalsifiable. Arm statistics are a
\emph{discounted} Welford state $(n,\mu,M_2)$ rather than raw sums, so variance
is numerically stable and old observations decay as machine and data change. Its
callers are the join-strategy choice---hash, broadcast, sort-merge---and the
staged-versus-one-shot routing decision below.

\textbf{An OLS two-line crossover} learns a \emph{threshold} where the bandit
learns a choice. It fits $t \approx a + bx$ per algorithm and solves for the
$x$---build bytes, or build rows---at which the cheaper-below algorithm is
overtaken by the cheaper-above one. The result is clamped to a band around the
shipped default, so one noisy early fit cannot send a threshold somewhere absurd,
and an ambiguous fit returns nothing and the caller keeps the default. Its
callers are the broadcast byte threshold and the sort-merge row crossover.

A third layer learns per-signature \emph{scalars}: how big each join side was,
how many rows a breaker shuffled, how far an aggregate collapsed its input,
whether partial pre-aggregation paid off.

\paragraph{The invariance that licenses all of it.} Every arm emits the same
relation; a threshold selects between equivalent algorithms; a partition count
only shards. Learning may change how much memory a query reserves, when it
spills, how wide a morsel is, and which of several equivalent algorithms runs,
and it may never change what the query returns. That is property-tested---tuned
run equals untuned run---and a cold store reproduces untuned behavior byte for
byte. Every read is best-effort: a malformed bucket, a degenerate fit, or a
missing key yields the default, never an exception into planning.

\subsection{When the loop measured nothing}
\label{sec:kyber-defects}

TPC-H sf10 was losing to DuckDB by $5$--$12\times$ on exactly the queries with
the most joins while single-table shapes sat at $1.2$--$1.7\times$. A split that
clean by query \emph{shape} is not a kernel problem. CPU-seconds against wall
time showed q8 using 4.3 of 96 cores where DuckDB used 22. All three causes were
in the control plane, and none produced a wrong answer.

An adaptive stage hands the next stage its intermediate wrapped in a source keyed
by \emph{object identity}---an object that dies with the execution. Both the
sketch writer and the statistics writer filed there anyway. The sketch was
recomputed every run (280M rows on q8's first execution); the learned store grew
one dead entry per execution without bound; and because a column absent from the
store is by definition measured-for-the-first-time, the learned \emph{generation}
advanced every run. The generation is part of the plan cache key, so the cache
never hit: zero hits, three misses, every run, forever. Q8 re-derived its plan,
join-order DP included, for 130\,ms per run against DuckDB's 84\,ms for the whole
query. Separately, the cache's calibration epoch counted in a different clock
from the refit it tracked, rolling over every second execution whether or not
anything had been refit. Fixed, q8's steady state fell from $\sim$340\,ms to
$\sim$165\,ms.

Third, whether to re-optimize between stages is a cost question and nothing was
measuring cost. The gate turned staging on when a join had a breaker-produced
operand whose size was a guess---at scale, nearly every multi-join query---priced
against an assumed 20--40\,ms of control plane. It is not that: the loop runs one
breaker per stage, so it materializes every join separately and forfeits both
operator fusion and the streaming executor's width. At sf10, q8 cost 887\,ms
staged against 142\,ms one-shot; q5 ran at $1.9\times$ parallelism on 96 cores
where the one-shot plan reached $22.6\times$. The gate is now a two-arm bandit
over \emph{staged} and \emph{one\_shot} rewarded with wall time, each arm's first
observation discarded because a shape's first run on a route pays costs the route
does not own.

\paragraph{The general lesson.} All three defects were silent, each producing
right answers while a load-bearing mechanism did nothing. A learning system needs
tests that assert \emph{the learning happened}---cache hit rates, generation
stability, sketch reuse---not only that the answers are right, because the
answers are right either way.

% ===========================================================================
\section{\carbo{}: resource decisions from measured footprints}
\label{sec:carbonite}
% ===========================================================================

\carbo{} decides whether a plan is feasible, hands out memory reservations and
shuffle credits, and decides when a query goes out of core. It never rewrites a
plan and never computes a result.

\subsection{Admission returns a counter-offer}

Conventional admission control answers a boolean: does this plan fit? A rejection
is useless to a planner, because it names no smaller thing to try. \carbo{}
returns the binding constraint \emph{and} a counter-offer---a smaller credit
window, a lower parallelism---that \kyber{} can re-plan around, and it names the
\emph{operator} that binds rather than the resource that ran out, so an
infeasible plan points at the join or aggregate to reshape. This follows from the
subsystem split: because \carbo{} may not rewrite the plan itself, the only
useful thing it can return is enough information for the layer that may.

\subsection{A learned memory model: a ratio, at P80}
\label{sec:learned-mem}

Core records each operator's actual peak bytes. Historically every sizing
decision---admission, spill, reservation, morsel width---sized from \kyber{}'s
plan estimate and never consulted what the operator really used. Two choices in
closing that gap are forced rather than tuned, and both are worth stating because
the obvious alternative is wrong in each case.

\textbf{Fit a per-input-row ratio, not an absolute peak.} A family's absolute
peak depends on query size---a 1M-row aggregate peaks far above a 10-row
one---so replaying a stored byte figure onto a differently-sized plan is simply
wrong. Fitting $\mathit{bytes\_per\_row}$ makes the figure size-general: multiply
by \emph{this} plan's estimated input rows. It is the same reason the cardinality
layer learns filter \emph{selectivity} rather than an absolute row count. One
blended figure then feeds five decisions through a single entry
point---whether to route out of core, how many partitions to shard spilled state
into, whether to compress it, what admission accounts against, and what
\texttt{reserve} charges---plus morsel sizing, which caps the row count by the
widest learned per-row width, so a workload of wide rows (large strings,
embeddings, blob handles) keeps its true byte working set inside budget where a
fixed row count would not.

\textbf{Fit the 80th percentile, not the median.} The median was the obvious
first choice and is the wrong statistic. Memory sizing is one-sided:
over-provisioning costs a little headroom, under-provisioning costs the process.
A median is by construction exceeded by half the runs, so fitting it means every
second execution of a family reserves less than it turned out to need---and the
runs that exceed it are exactly the large, skewed, wide-row ones that OOM. P80
rather than P95 because a family may have ten samples and the tail of a memory
distribution is fat and noisy: chasing a far tail on ten samples fits noise,
while P80 moves the fit off the coin-flip without doing that. The result is
clamped to a bounded factor of the plan estimate regardless, and a cold store is
an empty pass-through, so a first run is byte-for-byte the pre-learning behavior.

\subsection{Credits: static grant, live control, learned warm start}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=20mm] (p) at (-26,0) {Producer};
  \node[pfig/gate, minimum width=15mm, font=\scriptsize] (c) at (0,0) {credits\,$>0$?};
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=20mm] (r) at (26,0) {Reducer};
  \draw[pfig/arrow] (p) -- node[pfig/label,above=0.2mm]{\scriptsize batch} (c);
  \draw[pfig/arrow] (c) -- (r);
  \draw[pfig/arrowD] (r.north) to[out=110,in=70]
        node[pfig/label,pos=0.5]{\scriptsize credit returned} (c.north);
  \node[pfig/box, minimum width=23mm, align=left, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2.5pt]
       (l1) at (-26,-16) {\textbf{1.} static grant\\[-1pt]\tiny \carbo{}, clamped};
  \node[pfig/alt, minimum width=23mm, align=left, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2.5pt]
       (l2) at (0,-16) {\textbf{2.} AIMD\\[-1pt]\tiny live backpressure};
  \node[pfig/new, minimum width=23mm, align=left, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2.5pt]
       (l3) at (26,-16) {\textbf{3.} warm start\\[-1pt]\tiny learned per shape};
  \draw[pfig/arrowD] (l1.north) -- (p.south);
  \draw[pfig/arrowD] (l2.north) -- (c.south);
  \draw[pfig/arrowNew] (l3.north) -- (r.south);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Three layers govern one credit window. One credit is one in-flight batch
slot, so the window is a direct bound on a channel's memory. Learning moves only
the \emph{starting point}; the live AIMD controller still governs the window
actually used, which is why a stale learned value costs a few round trips of
convergence and never correctness.}
\label{fig:credits}
\end{figure}

Flow control is credit-based: one credit is one in-flight batch slot, and a
producer blocks at zero. Three layers sit on that (\cref{fig:credits}). \carbo{}
grants a static window---16 slots by default---and clamps any request to a
ceiling, so no channel starves the rest. An AIMD controller then grows and
shrinks the window per remote fetch from observed backpressure, additively up and
multiplicatively down, with hysteresis between a high and a low occupancy mark so
a channel does not oscillate at the threshold. And a recurring shuffle channel
warm-starts from the window past runs of that signature converged on, persisted
back after the run.

\subsection{Spill, governed on both sides}

Two things here are not obvious.

\textbf{Published shuffle output is memory nobody was reserving.} A mapper
produces a bucket, hands it to the node's Flight store, and it stays resident
until a reducer collects it---so with as many mappers as reducers, a node holds
its whole share of the shuffle against no reservation. The store now reserves
what it holds, against the same envelope every operator draws on, and writes its
largest buckets to disk when the pool cannot cover growth. That reservation also
makes published output the pool's \emph{first} spill victim, which is the right
choice: a published bucket is finished work waiting to be collected, so spilling
it stalls nobody, where spilling a half-built hash table interrupts an operator
still using it.

\textbf{Disk gets its own ladder, and buckets route away before it is full.} An
out-of-space write cannot be retried or degraded, so the local tier is
\emph{measured} rather than accounted---a volume filled by a co-tenant is seen as
readily as one this query filled---on three levels, and new buckets route to
remote storage at the middle level instead of waiting for the floor. The reason
is timing: a bucket's tier is fixed when it opens, so by the time the reserve
floor is crossed, several buckets can already be streaming to a volume that
cannot hold them.

\subsection{Three transport tiers, chosen with no configuration}

Bulk Arrow never enters the Ray object store; it moves over Arrow
Flight~\cite{arrowflight}. Within a shuffle a reducer's sources fall into three
tiers, selected automatically: same-process reads straight from the local store;
same-node-different-process memory-maps a 64-byte-aligned Arrow IPC file and
decodes zero-copy; another node takes a credit-bounded Flight stream. The
shared-memory tier is a second copy in tmpfs, so a mapper skips the mirror under
memory pressure and the reducer falls back to Flight---bit-identical, so
single-node-equals-distributed holds regardless. Measured on a cluster, an
8-producer-to-1-reducer gather runs at 33.6\,GB/s over shared memory against
4.5\,GB/s over loopback Flight: $7.5\times$ through the full concurrent gather,
$\sim$23$\times$ point to point.

% ===========================================================================
\section{Execution mechanisms}
\label{sec:mechanisms}
% ===========================================================================

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=1.02\columnwidth, height=3.9cm,
  ybar, bar width=15pt,
  ymode=log, ymin=300, ymax=60000, log origin=infty,
  ylabel={\scriptsize peak RSS (MB)},
  symbolic x coords={materializing,{+ output morsels},{+ input slicing}},
  xtick=data, xticklabel style={font=\scriptsize},
  ylabel style={font=\scriptsize}, yticklabel style={font=\scriptsize},
  nodes near coords, every node near coord/.append style={font=\scriptsize},
  nodes near coords style={/pgf/number format/1000 sep={,}},
  axis lines=left, ymajorgrids, grid style={pfigLight},
  enlarge x limits=0.22,
]
\addplot[fill=okOrange!55, draw=okOrange] coordinates
  {(materializing,13139) ({+ output morsels},5179) ({+ input slicing},728)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Bounding a streaming join's fan-out. A 16{,}384-row probe morsel against
a 20{,}000-row build side with one distinct key emits 327 million rows---as a
\emph{single} batch, because the probe assumed one morsel in implies one morsel
out. Morselizing the output halved it; slicing the probe on the input side, the
only thing that bounds the join-index arrays, took it the rest of the way.
Together $18\times$ less memory and $3.2\times$ less time, identical results.}
\label{fig:fanout}
\end{figure}

The executor pulls 16{,}384-row morsels through linear runs and materializes only
at breakers, in the style of morsel-driven parallelism~\cite{leis2014morsel}. On
the TPC-H q3/q4/q5 shape the streaming path holds 3.4\,MB at 1M rows and 3.3\,MB
at 4M, where the materializing path grows from 198\,MB to 794\,MB---and it is
$1.24\times$ faster, which is why it is the default.

\paragraph{The bound was stated, tested, and false.} \cref{fig:fanout} tells the
story. This was never a cartesian-only problem: an ordinary equi-join on a skewed
key hits it, because a build side holding 100{,}000 duplicates of one key turns
every probe row carrying it into 100{,}000 output rows in one batch. It survived
a 46-test operator oracle suite because every one of those tests is
\emph{correct}. A correctness suite cannot observe a memory property. We now
treat peak bytes as an operator-level assertion rather than a documented intent.

\paragraph{Short-circuiting a conjunction without a selection vector.}
Evaluating $c_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge c_n$ the obvious way computes every conjunct
over every row; a five-predicate filter keeping 1.65\% of rows pays five
full-width passes. DuckDB uses a selection vector. Arrow has none---a kernel
reads a whole array---so the equivalent is to \emph{compact}: evaluate the
cheapest conjunct at full width, gather the survivors of only the columns the
remaining conjuncts name, and evaluate the rest against that. Correctness needs
three parts, and the third needs a guard. Composition: ANDing truthy masks gives
the identical keep set under any nesting. Position independence: every kernel a
conjunct can reach is elementwise. And \emph{skipping cannot hide an error},
enforced by a syntactic predicate, exhaustive over the grammar with no wildcard
arm---only conjuncts whose failures are \emph{schema}-driven rather than
\emph{row}-driven are eligible, so a skipped row can never have been the row that
raised. Checked arithmetic, division, a strict \texttt{CAST}, and every
string-\emph{producing} function take the old path untouched. Measured on a
17-column \texttt{lineitem} shape, whole operator including the gather both paths
pay: TPC-H q6 $1.50\times$, a \texttt{LIKE} behind a cheap guard $2.03\times$, a
\texttt{regexp\_matches} behind one $5.73\times$.

\paragraph{Dictionary-native comparison.} Dictionary columns were decoded at the
leaf before any kernel ran. The rewrite compares the \emph{distinct values} and
gathers one bit per row through the keys, resting on a one-line identity---an
elementwise comparison commutes with a gather,
$\mathsf{cmp}(\mathsf{take}(v,k),\ell) = \mathsf{take}(\mathsf{cmp}(v,\ell),k)$---so
it is bit-identical by construction rather than by measurement, which is what
lets it live inside the correctness oracle. On 6M rows with 25 distinct values,
\texttt{m = 'AIR'} goes from 144.9\,ms to 7.4\,ms, $\mathbf{19.6\times}$, against
the same column \emph{already decoded}.

\paragraph{A runtime filter that crosses joins.} A hash join's build-side key set
is sunk down its probe pipeline and applied at the scan, where a row dies before
every predicate above it---semi-join reduction~\cite{bloom1970}. The placement
walk stopped at anything that was not a filter or pass-through projection,
including a join, which confines it to a star join whose fact table is the
immediate probe input. Real plans are not shaped that way. The walk now descends
through an \emph{inner} join into whichever side the output mapping says the
column comes from; soundness is the join's own algebra, since every output row of
$C \bowtie D$ takes a left-sourced value from exactly one row of $C$. Inner
only---an outer join manufactures nulls where the argument fails. TPC-H q5 at
sf10: $709 \rightarrow 378$\,ms, and 15.1 to 8.1 CPU-seconds in isolation.

\paragraph{A deterministic parallel dense join map.} When a build key's range is
tight the hash table is replaced by direct addressing. At sf10
\texttt{orders.o\_orderkey} is 15M rows over 60M slots---the build side of seven
TPC-H queries---and the fill was a serial loop behind a single-threaded 240\,MB
memset, so \texttt{lineitem} $\bowtie$ \texttt{orders} spent 2.38\,s of CPU across
209\,ms of wall time: 11 of 96 cores. The map is now cut into contiguous slot
ranges, each handed its build rows in ascending row order---the order the serial
loop visited them---so every collision chain comes out identical, ranges are
disjoint, and there is no synchronization and no \texttt{unsafe}. The join goes
$209 \rightarrow 118$\,ms against DuckDB's 125\,ms, parallelism rising 11.4 to
26.4 of 96 cores while total CPU barely moves.

\paragraph{Range joins.} Every inequality once lowered to a cartesian join with
the predicate as a filter above it: $|L|\times|R|$ rows however few survived.
IEJoin~\cite{khayyat2015iejoin} is now a real operator with both axis sorts and
the sweep parallelized. At 10{,}000 rows it went from 3{,}380\,ms to 3.7\,ms
against DuckDB's 9.5; at 100{,}000, from not running at all to 19\,ms against 56.
It reaches parity at a million and loses above it, because each left row scans
the axis-1 suffix from its own bound to the end, where DuckDB prunes block
\emph{pairs} whose key ranges cannot intersect.

