& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{sf1}} & & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{sf10}} & \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){5-6}
Baseline & geomean $b/x$ & wins & & geomean $b/x$ & wins & Note \\
\midrule
DuckDB (native compressed store) & 1.02$\times$ & 12/22 & & \loss 1.49$\times$ & \loss 3/22 & storage gap; \cref{sec:limits} \\
DuckDB (same Arrow input)        & --- & --- & & \win \textbf{0.53}$\times$ & \win \textbf{21/22} & like-for-like$^*$ \\
Polars~\cite{polars}             & \win \textbf{0.55}$\times$ & \win 19/22 & & \win \textbf{0.45}$\times$ & \win 17/22 & \\
Daft~\cite{daft}                 & \win \textbf{0.48}$\times$ & \win 18/20 & & \win \textbf{0.52}$\times$ & 13/20 & wrong answers on q6, q15$^\dagger$ \\
Spark~\cite{armbrust2015sparksql}& 0.041$\times$ & 22/22 & & 0.074$\times$ & 22/22 & reads Parquet in timed region$^\ddagger$ \\
Ray Data~\cite{raydata}          & 0.0015$\times$ & 22/22 & & 0.0053$\times$ & 22/22 & not a relational engine$^\S$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{TPC-H, all 22 queries, correctness-gated, one 96-core node. $b/x$ is
$\text{\sys{}}_{ms}/x_{ms}$: \textbf{below 1.00 means \sys{} is faster.} Suite
totals (ms), sf1 / sf10 --- \sys{} 618 / 4{,}491; DuckDB 616 / 2{,}353; Polars
1{,}256 / 10{,}624; Daft 1{,}224 / 9{,}261; Spark 14{,}555 / 57{,}781.
$^*$Separate run: DuckDB executing the same zero-copy Arrow \sys{} is given,
rather than a compressed store it was allowed to build first; 4{,}453 vs
8{,}436\,ms. $^\dagger$Daft covers the 20 queries it can express, raising on q21
and q22; \sys{} agrees with DuckDB on 22 of 22 at both scales. $^\ddagger$Spark
and Ray Data read Parquet inside the timed region where the other four receive an
in-memory Arrow handle, so their ratios are overstated by an unisolated scan
cost. $^\S$\cref{sec:raydata}.}
\label{tab:tpch}
\end{table*}

\paragraph{Setup.} One Intel Xeon Platinum 8275CL at 3.00\,GHz, 96 vCPU,
184\,GiB, Linux 6.8; distributed results on an 8-node Ray cluster, 128 vCPU; GPU
results on 8$\times$T4. DuckDB 1.5.5, Polars 1.36.1, Daft 0.7.21, Ray 2.56.0,
PySpark 4.2.0, PyArrow 19.0.1. The harness refuses to report a timing for a query
whose result does not match DuckDB, compared as an order-independent multiset
with $\mathrm{atol}=10^{-6}$, $\mathrm{rtol}=10^{-9}$. Timings are best-of-$N$
warm.

Two hazards are worth naming because both cost real time. A shared machine is not
a benchmark machine: at load average 16--41 a repeated run swung $\pm$25\%, so
claims moving less than $2\times$ are quoted from a run at load under 5. And an
in-place rebuild of the compiled extension silently replaces the engine under a
running benchmark, so we stamp the shared object's mtime around every run and
discard any run in which it moved.

\subsection{Does the algebra remove the fork?}

The central claim is architectural, so its evidence is a testing argument and we
state its strength precisely. \cref{eq:merge} is property-tested over random
partitionings; an integration test asserts single-node equals multi-partition;
and an operator matrix crosses $\{$collect, spill, iterate, distributed$\}$ with
$\{$nulls, empty, one row, duplicates, $-0.0$/NaN, descending$\}$, because the
defects that reach production here are combinations of a non-default flag with a
non-default path.

Two admissions. A test suite is not a proof. And the argument binds only
operators that went through the admission criterion---we found one place it had
not: a watermarked windowed aggregate passed a distributability gate that never
checked the watermark, so the same query returned different results single-node
and distributed. The gate now refuses those plans loudly. The algebra removes the
fork \emph{within} the operator set; the residual exposure is at planner
admission boundaries, which are ordinary code needing ordinary tests.

\subsection{TPC-H}

\cref{tab:tpch} is the headline. Against Polars and Daft, \sys{} leads by
$1.8$--$2.2\times$ in geometric mean at both scales. Against DuckDB the result
splits precisely along the line \cref{tab:differs} predicted: on identical Arrow
input \sys{} wins 21 of 22 at sf10 and is $1.89\times$ faster overall; against
DuckDB's compressed native store it loses. q1 and q6 are essentially scans and
sit at $1.47\times$/$1.52\times$, about what reading compressed pages instead of
raw Arrow buys. But q21 ($3.18\times$), q9 ($3.79\times$), q7 ($3.19\times$), and
q5 ($3.95\times$) are several times that, and those are engine work.

Correctness is not free for everyone. Daft returns a wrong TPC-H q6 result at
both scales, dropping the \texttt{l\_discount = 0.07} rows because $0.06+0.01$ in
IEEE double is $0.06999999999999999$. Ground truth was computed independently in
PyArrow and equals the official TPC-H answer; Polars makes the same mistake. This
is why every timing is gated: a benchmark that does not check results reports the
fastest wrong answer.

