\caption{Operator mix at sf1, $\text{\sys{}}_{ms}/x_{ms}$; below 1.00 is a \sys{}
win. All engines take the same in-memory Arrow input, so this carries none of
\cref{tab:tpch}'s scan asymmetry. PyArrow (Acero) has no window functions.}
\label{tab:opmix}
\end{table}

\cref{tab:opmix} is the cleanest comparison in the paper---one input
representation, one machine, no SQL front-end differences, no scan in the timed
region for anybody. \sys{} sweeps PyArrow's Acero engine on all seven cases it
can express and takes 8 of 11 from DuckDB and 9 of 11 from Polars.

On ClickBench~\cite{clickbench} (43 queries, correctness-gated) \sys{} wins 43 of
43 against DuckDB on identical Arrow input, 35 of 40 against Polars---the five
losses are all sub-3\,ms queries where fixed per-query overhead dominates---and
26 of 43 against DuckDB's native store. The losses are diagnostic rather than
diffuse: essentially all are high-cardinality grouping and
\texttt{COUNT(DISTINCT)} over string or near-unique keys, where the group count
approaches the row count and per-thread partials stop collapsing. That is the
same shape behind the two remaining TPC-H losses, so it is one named work item,
not a general deficit.

\subsection{Scaling out}
\label{sec:scaleout}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=1.02\columnwidth, height=4.2cm,
  xlabel={\scriptsize worker nodes}, ylabel={\scriptsize aggregate GB/s},
  xlabel style={font=\scriptsize}, ylabel style={font=\scriptsize},
  xtick={2,4,8}, xticklabels={2,4,8},
  xmin=1.4, xmax=8.8, ymin=0, ymax=17,
  xticklabel style={font=\scriptsize}, yticklabel style={font=\scriptsize},
  axis lines=left, ymajorgrids, grid style={pfigLight},
  legend style={font=\scriptsize, draw=none, fill=none, at={(0.03,0.97)},
                anchor=north west, cells={anchor=west}},
]
\addplot[draw=pfigGray, dashed, line width=0.7pt, mark=none]
  coordinates {(2,2.0) (4,4.0) (8,8.0)};
\addlegendentry{linear from 2 nodes}
\addplot[draw=okBlue, line width=1.2pt, mark=*, mark options={fill=okBlue, scale=0.8}]
  coordinates {(2,2.0) (4,6.9) (8,15.2)};
\addlegendentry{measured}
\node[font=\scriptsize, text=okBlue, anchor=west] at (axis cs:8.05,15.2) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Aggregate all-to-all shuffle throughput against cluster width, with a
linear reference anchored at two nodes. Four times the hardware returns
$7.6\times$ the throughput. The mechanism is the algebra: mappers publish
pre-aggregated state rather than rows, so a hierarchical combiner tree plus
credit-based flow control keeps per-node memory bounded as the cluster widens,
and adding nodes adds reduce capacity instead of contention. A \emph{single}
reducer stays NIC-bound at $\sim$2.7\,GB/s, so the gain is in the aggregate,
where every node reduces at once.}
\label{fig:scaling}
\end{figure}

\cref{fig:scaling} is the scaling result. It is superlinear rather than merely
linear, and the reason is worth separating from the headline: at two nodes the
shuffle does not saturate the available reduce capacity, so part of the gain from
2 to 4 is recovered inefficiency rather than new throughput. The claim we stand
behind is the shape---adding nodes adds throughput without a per-node memory
penalty---not a promise of $1.9\times$ per doubling forever.

The single-query picture matches. On TPC-H sf10 q6 with every engine reading the
same S3 Parquet and correctness-gated, \sys{} over 64 partitions runs 224.5\,ms
against Daft on its Ray runner at 535.8\,ms and DuckDB-on-Arrow single-node at
457.3\,ms---and Daft returns the wrong answer described above. Across a pipeline
suite at sf1/sf10/sf100, \sys{} leads Ray Data on every pipeline, and leads Daft
on joins ($1.7$--$2.2\times$), group-by, and metadata-only counts.

\paragraph{Where linear scaling stops, measured.} Two ceilings bound the claim
and both are ours.

\emph{Shuffle cost is quadratic in partition count.} On a forced distributed
6M-row grouped sum, 16 partitions cost 1{,}031\,ms and 128 cost 93{,}189\,ms.
Eight times the partitions cost roughly ninety times the time---close to the
$64\times$ a $P^2$ pair count predicts---so there is a fixed cost of about
5.7\,ms per partition \emph{pair}. ``One partition per cluster CPU'' is the
obvious way to size a distributed run and is exactly the setting that falls off
the cliff. This bounds the fan-out a very large run can use.

\emph{Distributed scan throughput does not scale with the data.} On the
object-store read path, aggregate throughput across 8 workers measures roughly
90\,MB/s and is approximately \emph{constant} from sf10 to sf100, where a
competitor driving more parallel coalesced range reads per node is several times
faster on the same corpus. So the linear-scaling claim in this section is a claim
about the \emph{shuffle}, which is the part the algebra governs. The read path in
front of it is a separate piece of work, it is not yet good, and we would rather
say so than quote the shuffle number as though it characterized an end-to-end
petabyte run.

\subsection{GPU and ML workloads}
\label{sec:raydata}

This is Ray Data's design target, so it is the comparison that carries weight,
and the ratios need attributing to mechanisms rather than reported as a
scoreboard. On 8$\times$T4 with real models: ResNet-50 batch inference
$2.05\times$, image embeddings $1.98\times$, fractional-GPU packing $1.96\times$,
video-clip inference $3.6\times$, training-data ingest $3.0\times$, multimodal
JPEG-to-GPU $1.3$--$2\times$.

Three larger figures---audio feature extraction $12.5\times$, LLM batch inference
$11.1\times$, text embeddings $47\times$---are not general execution speed and
should not be read as such. They come from two mechanisms, both scheduling rather
than kernels: a \emph{session-warm actor pool}, where the model loads once per
session and is reused across executions, so the ratio scales with how much of the
job is model load and shrinks toward the numbers above as jobs grow; and
\emph{native in-engine preprocessing}, where mel-spectrogram and MFCC run as
Arrow kernels rather than a per-batch Python UDF. Both are choices Ray Data could
adopt. The durable claim is the $2$--$3.5\times$ range.

Two honest exceptions. On a single maximally-large compute-bound job both engines
saturate the same GPU at the same FLOPs and the ratio is near parity. And our own
image ingest began this work \emph{losing} to Ray Data and Daft at
$\sim$350\,img/s, because our decode kernels ran serially and the executor sized
its pool from the morsel count; five fixes took it to $\sim$5{,}700\,img/s.

\paragraph{On the TPC-H Ray Data column.} Ray Data is not a relational engine and
does not claim to be: no optimizer, no join reordering, no pushdown. Our 22
queries are hand-written against its imperative API, so that column also measures
our fluency in someone else's toolkit, and it reads Parquet inside the timed
region. We report it for completeness and draw no headline from it. A prior
version of our adapter registered each table as a single block---Ray Data's unit
of parallelism---so a 6M-row table ran on one core of a 96-core box; that was a
harness defect, it is fixed, and every number here is post-fix.

% ===========================================================================
\section{What is not done yet}
\label{sec:limits}
% ===========================================================================

We would rather hand a reader a roadmap than a scoreboard, so this section is
specific about what does not work yet. Every item below is measured, and each one
is localized to a named piece of work rather than a general deficit.

\textbf{The adaptive loop costs 20\% on TPC-H today.} At sf10, with both routes
given their own learned state, \texttt{adaptive=False} beats
\texttt{adaptive="auto"} on 20 of 22 queries: 3{,}889\,ms against 4{,}669\,ms. The
cause is diagnosable, not fundamental---the loop materializes one breaker per
stage, forfeiting fusion and executor width, and the gate that turns it on reads
the provenance \emph{label} of \cref{sec:provenance} rather than the error
history the hub already collects. We report the loss rather than disabling the
feature and quoting the better number.

\textbf{We are not finer-grained than Spark AQE.} An earlier version of our own
documentation claimed otherwise. It is stage-boundary adaptation with the same
granularity, and it is off below 20M input rows. The defensible claim is
narrower: stage-boundary re-optimization available single-node as well as
distributed, \emph{plus} a cross-query learned loop neither DuckDB nor Spark has.

\textbf{Storage is the comparator we do not sweep.} 3 of 22 TPC-H at sf10, 26 of
43 ClickBench, 6 of 11 operators---all against DuckDB's native store, and on the
same Arrow input \sys{} wins essentially all of them. The residue is compression,
zone-map skipping, and late materialization: a storage-engine program, and it
should be costed as one.

\textbf{q21 is an algorithm gap.} It spends $3.8\times$ DuckDB's CPU while using
\emph{more} of the machine, so parallelism cannot fix it. We tried pushing a
semi-join into its decorrelated aggregate; input fell $12.4\times$ and it was
still a loss, because the semi-join is a full pass over the same 60M rows. The
aggregate has to stop being a 15M-group aggregate, which needs a residual-capable
mark join we do not have.

\textbf{A cold query shape costs 5--8\,ms of optimizer, in Python.} Of a 5.34\,ms
optimize, one phase carrying 282 rules costs 4.19\,ms. Four attempts to remove it
produced no change, 3\%, and one regression, all reverted; the cost is 188 node
rules across 19 traversals with nothing dominant. It is architectural, not a hot
spot. A repeated shape hits the plan cache at 0.12\,ms.

\textbf{Streaming is micro-batch}~\cite{zaharia2013dstreams} and cannot express
Flink's guarantees~\cite{carbone2015flink}: no operator graph, no barriers, a
single global watermark on the driver, and state as one in-memory Arrow batch
rewritten in full each micro-batch. Also open: no
\texttt{StringView}~\cite{neumann2020umbra}, dictionaries do not survive project
or join, shuffle is RAM-only so a lost worker triggers lineage recompute, and
task granularity is roughly one task per node rather than per partition.

% ===========================================================================
\section{Related work}
% ===========================================================================

\sys{}'s execution is morsel-driven~\cite{leis2014morsel} over a vectorized Arrow
representation in the MonetDB/X100 lineage~\cite{boncz2005monetdb}, with a
compiled path for scalar expressions in the spirit
of~\cite{neumann2011compiling} but restricted to a subset with a
proven-identical interpreter fallback. Volcano~\cite{graefe1994volcano}
established the operator/exchange separation; the departure is that \sys{} has no
distributed operator to separate. Partitioned hash joins on multi-core hardware
are well studied~\cite{balkesen2013mainmemory}; our wrinkle is determinism.

Mid-query re-optimization~\cite{kabra1998midquery}, progressive
optimization~\cite{markl2004progressive}, eddies~\cite{avnur2000eddies}, and the
survey in~\cite{deshpande2007adaptive} are the lineage of the stage-boundary
loop, with Spark AQE~\cite{sparkaqe} its closest deployed relative. Learned
optimizers~\cite{marcus2019neo,marcus2021bao,kipf2019learned} attack estimation
with models; \sys{} takes the lighter route of
sketches~\cite{flajolet2007hyperloglog,karnin2016kll,cormode2005countmin}, refit
cost coefficients, and a bandit~\cite{auer2002ucb} over result-equivalent
strategies, which buys reproducibility and a cold start that is exactly the
untuned system. \cite{leis2015goodjoin} is the standing argument for why measured
cardinalities beat better estimators. The epoch permutation used for
training-data shuffling is a Feistel network~\cite{luby1988feistel} with cycle
walking~\cite{black2002ciphers}, the same idea as~\cite{mosaicstreaming}, giving
an $O(1)$-memory shuffled order with mid-epoch resume.

% ===========================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ===========================================================================

The architectures most teams run were designed for a machine that was too small,
a schema that never changed, and a world where machine learning happened
somewhere else. All three assumptions have expired, and the workarounds they
force---a cluster kept warm so small queries are bearable, a shuffle partition
count tuned by hand, a second copy of the data for the ML team---have become so
familiar that they read as the cost of doing business.

\sys{} is an argument that they are not. Requiring every stateful operator to be
one mergeable triple removes the split between the fast engine and the scalable
one: the same code runs on a laptop and on a cluster, so speed does not have to
be re-earned at scale and scale does not have to be paid for in advance. That
same constraint makes measurement transferable, which is what lets the engine
plan from evidence---cost coefficients refit from real runtimes, join strategies
chosen from real outcomes, memory sized from real footprints---rather than from
constants chosen years ago for hardware nobody is running. And because decode,
embedding, and inference are expressions in that same algebra, the pipeline that
turns raw data into model input is one optimized plan instead of three systems
and two copies.

What the design does not buy is storage. \sys{} matches DuckDB at sf1 and loses
at sf10, and the gap is compression, zone maps, and late materialization---a
program of work the algebra neither helps nor hinders. The adaptive loop is a net
cost on TPC-H today for a reason we can name and fix. We report both, because a
system that is honest about its ceilings is easier to trust about its floors, and
because both are now localized to specific work rather than to the idea.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} Every number comes from a committed harness:
\texttt{benchmarks/run.py} drives the engine adapters and \texttt{harness.py}
enforces the correctness gate, refusing to time any query whose result does not
match DuckDB. The harness records the engine build profile with each run, so a
result cannot silently be attributed to a different build. Open items before
archival publication: \cref{tab:tpch} reports a single run per scale and needs
repetitions with confidence intervals, and no ablation yet attributes the
end-to-end total to the individual mechanisms of \cref{sec:mechanisms}.

